 the subrange from the start of the range","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"expression until the end of the array."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","code":["let streets = [\"Adams\", \"Bryant\", \"Channing\", \"Douglas\", \"Evarts\"]","let streetsSlice = streets[2...]","print(streetsSlice)","\/\/ [\"Channing\", \"Douglas\", \"Evarts\"]"],"type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The accessed slice uses the same indices for the same elements as the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"original collection uses. This example searches "},{"code":"streetsSlice","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" for one"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"of the strings in the slice, and then uses that index in the original"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"array."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","code":["let index = streetsSlice.firstIndex(of: \"Evarts\")    \/\/ 4","print(streets[index!])","\/\/ \"Evarts\""],"type":"codeListing"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Always use the slice’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"startIndex"},{"text":" property instead of assuming that its","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"indices start at a particular value. Attempting to access an element by","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"using an index outside the bounds of the slice’s indices may result in a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"runtime error, even if that index is valid for the original collection.","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","code":["print(streetsSlice.startIndex)","\/\/ 2","print(streetsSlice[2])","\/\/ \"Channing\"","","print(streetsSlice[0])","\/\/ error: Index out of bounds"],"type":"codeListing"},{"style":"note","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"O(1)"}]}],"type":"aside","name":"Complexity"}]}],"references":{"doc://com.apple.Swift/documentatio